course: biology
lesson: 58
title: Flowers and pollination
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A flower is not there to look pretty for us: its job is to make seeds, and for that, pollen from the stamens must reach the carpel in the middle, usually of another flower of the same kind; that delivery is pollination.
explain: Plants cannot walk to each other, so they hire couriers and pay them in food: a bumblebee visiting foxgloves drinks sweet nectar, and comes out with yellow pollen on its legs and furry body, ready for the next flower.
explain: Every flower advertises to its own customers: bright colours and runway lines for bees, long red tubes for hummingbirds, pale petals and strong evening scent for moths, and even a stink of rotting meat for flies.
explain: Grasses do not bother with any of that: they throw millions of tiny pollen grains into the wind, which is why so many people sneeze with hay fever in June.
explain: Cartoons show bees making honey by squeezing flowers, or a single sunflower as one big flower; real bees turn nectar into honey inside the hive, and a sunflower head is hundreds of tiny flowers packed together.
fact: A flower has petals, stamens that make pollen and a carpel in the middle holding tiny ovules, which become seeds once pollen reaches the carpel.
fact: Bees drink nectar and collect pollen for their young; pollen sticks to their hairy bodies and is carried to the next flower, and that is pollination; fruit trees such as apples need insects at the blossom, and without them far fewer apples form.
fact: Bees see ultraviolet light, so they see patterns on petals that we cannot, but they do not see red well; birds see red clearly, so many red flowers are pollinated by birds.
fact: Hummingbirds drink nectar from long tube-shaped flowers and get pollen on their heads; their wings beat about 50 to 80 times a second.
fact: Grasses and trees like birch and hazel are pollinated by the wind; they make huge amounts of light pollen and have small, green, unscented flowers.
fact: The titan arum from Sumatra sends up a flower spike up to about 3 metres tall that smells of rotting meat, attracting beetles and flies; it opens only for a day or two.
fact: Evening primrose and honeysuckle open in the evening with pale petals, which show up best in dim light, and a strong scent, and are pollinated by night-flying moths.
fact: A sunflower head is hundreds or thousands of tiny flowers, each making one seed, arranged in spirals whose numbers are often 34, 55 or 89.
fact: Some orchids look like female insects to trick males into visiting; there are about 28000 kinds of orchid, and in Britain the bee orchid usually pollinates itself.
fact: A fig is a hollow ball of tiny flowers turned inwards, and wild figs are pollinated by tiny fig wasps that crawl inside; the fig digests any wasp that dies in it, and many figs sold in shops grow without wasps at all.
fact: A dandelion head is about 100 to 200 tiny flowers; it opens in bright daylight and closes at night or in rain, protecting its pollen.
fact: Honeybees turn nectar into honey by adding special substances and fanning it with their wings to dry it, as food for the hive in winter; honey is so dry and sugary that germs cannot grow in it, so it keeps for years.
fact: Bumblebees can buzz at a special pitch that shakes pollen out of tomato flowers, and farmers use them in greenhouses.
exercise: What is the main job of a flower? | To make seeds | To feed bees for free | To make the air smell nice | To catch rain for its roots
exercise: When you pull apart a tulip, what part makes the pollen? | The stamens | The carpel in the middle | The coloured petals | The green stem
exercise: What is pollination? | Pollen being carried to the carpel of a flower | A seed starting to grow under the soil in spring | A flower opening in the morning | A bee making honey
exercise: Why does a bumblebee visiting foxgloves end up with yellow pollen on its legs? | Pollen sticks to its hairy body as it collects nectar and pollen | It paints itself yellow to look like a wasp and scare off the birds | It rolls in pollen to keep warm | It steals pollen to build its nest
exercise: Which colour do bees see poorly? | Red | Blue | Yellow | Ultraviolet
exercise: What can bees see on petals that we cannot? | Ultraviolet patterns | Tiny writing on the petals | Heat from the sun on the petals | Raindrops before they fall
exercise: Why are many long red flowers visited by hummingbirds? | Birds see red clearly and their long bills reach the nectar | Hummingbirds are red themselves, so they like flowers of the same colour | Red flowers have no nectar for bees | Hummingbirds eat red petals
exercise: Why does the flowering grass in June make people sneeze? | Grass is pollinated by the wind and throws out clouds of light pollen | Grass flowers spray a stinging liquid | Bees bring grass dust to people's noses on their legs and drop it there | Grass gives off smoke in hot weather
exercise: Why does the titan arum at the botanic garden smell of rotting meat? | To attract beetles and flies that carry its pollen | To keep bees away from its nectar so it can save it all | Because it is dying | To scare away gardeners
exercise: Why does evening primrose open in the evening? | It is pollinated by night-flying moths | It is scared of the midday sun | It needs moonlight to make food | It cannot see in daylight
exercise: What is a sunflower head really? | Hundreds of tiny flowers packed together | One giant flower that makes only one big seed | A bunch of leaves | A fruit full of honey
exercise: How does a bee orchid get visits from insects? | It looks like a female insect, tricking males | It smells of chocolate | It hums like a bee | It traps them under a lid until they have rubbed off pollen
exercise: What is a fig, which you cut open at dinner? | A hollow ball of tiny flowers turned inwards | A single big seed | A berry that grew without any flowers | A root grown into a ball
exercise: Who pollinates wild figs? | Tiny fig wasps that crawl inside | Hummingbirds with long bills | The wind | Bats only
exercise: When does a dandelion flower close? | At night or when it rains | Only in winter | When a bee lands on it | Every hour
exercise: How do honeybees turn nectar into honey? | They add special substances and fan it with their wings to dry it | They squeeze the flowers hard and carry the juice straight into the hive | They leave it in the sun on the petals | They mix it with pollen and water
test: cartoon bee squeezes a flower and honey squirts out lol. that how honey works? | No, bees carry nectar to the hive and turn it into honey there | Yes, flowers are full of ready honey, the bee just squeezes it out | Yes, but only sunflowers | No, honey comes from bee eggs
test: What would happen to apple crops if there were no bees or other insects to visit the blossom? | Far fewer apples would form | More apples would grow | Apples would grow without seeds but just as many | The trees would grow pears instead
test: Why might a plant pollinated by moths have white flowers? | Pale petals show up best in the dim evening light | Moths eat only white food | White flowers are cheaper to make than coloured ones | Moths are scared of colours
test: Why do grasses not bother with bright petals? | The wind carries their pollen, and the wind does not see colour | Grasses are too poor to make colour | Bright petals would be eaten by cows | Grass petals are hidden underground
test: Why would a flower want only one kind of visitor? | So pollen goes to a flower of the same kind, not wasted on others | It does not like crowds | It runs out of nectar far too fast when lots of different insects come | Other visitors steal the petals
test: A sunflower head has 55 spirals one way. What does that tell you? | Its tiny flowers and seeds are packed in a special spiral pattern | It is 55 days old | It has 55 seeds only | It has been visited by 55 bees, and each one left behind a spiral track
test: What would happen to an orchid that relies on one insect if that insect died out? | It could not be pollinated and could slowly die out too | It would grow its own wings | Any other insect would take over the job at once, no problem | Nothing, orchids do not need pollen
test: why the hell does that flower stink like a bin full of dead meat?! | It attracts flies and beetles that carry its pollen | It is rotting and dying | Someone put meat in the pot as a joke | It is warning people not to touch it, because it is very poisonous
test: Why would a flower close when it rains? | To keep its pollen dry and safe until the sun comes back | Because the petals drown | To catch the raindrops inside | To trap the insects that are sheltering inside it from the rain
test: Mate reckons you're eating dead wasps in every fig. True? | Not really: many shop figs need no wasps, and wild figs digest them | Yes, each fig holds hundreds of live wasps | Yes, that is why figs are crunchy | No, wasps never go near figs, they hate the smell
test: Why does honey keep for years without going bad? | It is so dry and sugary that germs cannot grow in it | Bees put salt in it | The jar keeps it cold | It doesn't, it goes bad after a week, just like milk left out of the fridge
test: How would a moth find a flower in the dark? | By its strong scent and pale colour | By its loud buzzing | By its heat in the dark | By following bees
exam: Identify the floral structure responsible for the production of pollen. | The stamen | The carpel | The petal | The sepal
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the pollination of grasses? | It is effected by wind, with copious production of light pollen | It is effected exclusively by honeybees | It is effected by hummingbirds attracted to red flowers with long tubes | It does not take place at any time
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the head of the sunflower. | It comprises hundreds of small flowers, each producing a single seed | It constitutes a single flower bearing one large seed at its centre | It is composed of leaves arranged in spirals | It contains no seeds whatsoever
exam: It is given that bees perceive red poorly. Determine which pollinator is most probably served by a long red tubular flower. | A hummingbird | A small striped honeybee | The wind blowing past | A night-flying moth
